==English==
{{wp}}

===Etymology===
From {{af|en|auto-|-cracy|id1=self}}, from {{der|en|grc|αὐτοκρατία|t=A system of government by one person with absolute power.}}, from {{m|grc|αὐτός|t=single, self, same, alone}} + {{m|grc|κράτος|t=power}} + {{m|grc|-ία|t=feminine abstract nouns suffix}}; see also {{der|en|grc|αὐτοκρατής|t=one who governs alone}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɔːˈtɒkɹəsi/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-autocracy.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɒkɹəsi|s=4}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|uncountable}} A form of [[government]] in which unlimited power is held by a single [[individual]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=John Avlon|title=Opinion: The tide is turning against autocracy|magazine=w:CNN|url=https://www.cnn.com/2022/12/01/opinions/democracy-autocracy-russia-china-iran-avlon/index.html|date=2022-12-01|passage=I want to be clear: This shift toward democracy and away from '''autocracy''' is no reason to break out the “mission accomplished” banner.}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} An instance of this government.
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=John Avlon|title=Opinion: The tide is turning against autocracy|magazine=w:CNN|url=https://www.cnn.com/2022/12/01/opinions/democracy-autocracy-russia-china-iran-avlon/index.html|date=2022-12-01|passage=The will of the people of Ukraine to defend their democracy is a reminder that '''autocracies''' cannot ultimately overcome free people willing to defend their liberty.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|journal=w:The Christian Science Monitor|date=2025-05-09|author=Cameron Joseph|title=They’ve watched democracies fail. They see it happening under Trump.|titleurl=https://www.csmonitor.com/USA/Politics/2025/0509/donald-trump-authoritarian-playbook-democracy|passage=Among those who study authoritarian regimes, a consensus has emerged that [US president Donald Trump's] early moves display common characteristics of burgeoning '''autocracies.'''}}

